From 3a385ff94b636ac267d8e3827997b6af23d701a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: WXbet <57314510+WXbet@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 23:25:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] cache_flush: do not flush ahead of time on kernels before
 v4.3

Writing one second beyond the current time relies on the kernel keeping
newly added cache entries valid across a flush, which arrived in v4.3
with 778620364ef5 ("sunrpc/cache: make cache flushing more reliable.")
by pushing their last_refresh past the flush time.  The same commit also
made a flush that writes the current second reliable, and since v4.16
the value written here is ignored altogether.

On older kernels neither applies.  There cache_is_expired() treats every
entry whose last_refresh is not newer than flush_time as expired, so
flushing one second ahead expires the entries mountd writes immediately
afterwards in cache_export() for the request it is serving.  The
nfsd.export entry never becomes valid, nfsd denies the access, and every
NFSv3 mount fails with "access denied by server".  A Dreambox DM920
set-top box on its 3.14 vendor kernel does exactly that with nfs-utils
2.9.2, while 2.5.3 still works on the same box with the same
configuration.

Keep today's behaviour where the kernel supports it and write the
current second otherwise, which is what nfs-utils did before 7a4e2d1d
("Remove 'force' arg from cache_flush()").

Signed-off-by: WXbet <57314510+WXbet@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 support/export/cache_flush.c | 14 +++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/support/export/cache_flush.c b/support/export/cache_flush.c
index 046e6917..d28d39f9 100644
--- a/support/export/cache_flush.c
+++ b/support/export/cache_flush.c
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 
 #include "nfslib.h"
 #include "xlog.h"
+#include "version.h"
 
 extern int no_netlink;
 
@@ -136,12 +137,15 @@ static void cache_proc_flush(void)
 	};
 	now = time(0);
 
-	/* Since v4.16-rc2-3-g3b68e6ee3cbd the timestamp written is ignored.
-	 * It is safest always to flush caches if there is any doubt.
-	 * For earlier kernels, writing the next second from now is
-	 * the best we can do.
+	/* Kernels from v4.3 keep entries added after a flush valid and from
+	 * v4.16 ignore this timestamp, but older ones expire everything whose
+	 * last_refresh is not newer than it -- including the entries written
+	 * right afterwards for the request being served.
 	 */
-	sprintf(stime, "%" PRId64 "\n", (int64_t)now+1);
+	if (linux_version_code() >= MAKE_VERSION(4, 3, 0))
+		now++;
+
+	sprintf(stime, "%" PRId64 "\n", (int64_t)now);
 	for (c=0; cachelist[c]; c++) {
 		int fd;
 		sprintf(path, "/proc/net/rpc/%s/flush", cachelist[c]);
-- 
